    "en": {
      "prompt": "The rule f(x)=2^x maps real inputs to outputs. Which set is its range?",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Incorrectly counting the limiting value 0 would give range [0,+∞)"
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Because 2^x stays positive, the range is (0,+∞)"
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "For base 2, confusing the input set with outputs would give (-∞,+∞)"
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Mistaking 2^0=1 for a lower bound would give range (1,+∞)"
        }
      ],
      "explanation": "First test nonpositive outputs: 2^x=0 has no real solution and f(x)>0. Next take any y>0 and set x=log_2(y); then f(x)=y. The range is therefore (0,+∞).",
      "knowledgeTags": [
        "Elementary functions"
